 "standard_url": "https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/sboe-rules-tac/sboe-tac-currently-in-effect/ch111a.pdf",
 "standard_text": "4.4B determine products of a number and 10 or 100 using properties of operations and place value understandings",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 4 worksheets for Texas TEKS 4.4B: multiply by 10 and 100, missing factors, multiply tens and hundreds with facts, compare products and word problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "× 10 moves each digit one place left; × 100 moves it two places.",
 "key_sub": "7 × 300 = (7 × 3) × 100 = 2,100.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Multiplying by <b>10</b> makes each digit worth 10 times as much: every digit moves one place to the left and a 0 fills the ones place. 46 × 10 = 460.</li><li>Multiplying by <b>100</b> moves every digit two places to the left: 46 × 100 = 4,600.</li><li>Use facts and properties: 7 × 300 = (7 × 3) × 100 = 2,100.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>58 × 100</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Each digit moves two places left.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 5,800</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>6 × 400</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>6 × 4 = 24</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>24 × 100 = 2,400</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 2,400</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adding 100 instead of multiplying: 58 × 100 = 158</td><td>× 100 makes the number 100 times as large.</td></tr><tr><td>Writing only one zero for × 100</td><td>100 has two zeros: move the digits two places.</td></tr><tr><td>Mixing up 10 and 100</td><td>Count the zeros in the factor.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does TEKS 4.4B ask?",
   "a": "Determine products of a number and 10 or 100 using properties of operations and place value understandings."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why does multiplying by 10 add a zero?",
   "a": "Every digit moves to a place worth 10 times as much, so the ones place becomes 0."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you multiply 8 × 700?",
   "a": "8 × 7 = 56, then 56 × 100 = 5,600."
  },
  {
   "q": "Does this work for big numbers?",
   "a": "Yes. 2,345 × 100 = 234,500."
  }
